  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\nWhen `CURLOPT_CRLF` is enabled, `imap_perform_append()` advertises the **original** input file size in the IMAP `APPEND ... {N}` literal but then transmits the **expanded** body (bare LF → CRLF). The server consumes exactly N bytes as the literal, and the overflow bytes are parsed as new IMAP commands in the same authenticated session.\n\nThis is a protocol framing bug — curl tells the server \"here come N bytes,\" then sends more than N — not a case of the user injecting CRLFs into a protocol field. The bytes originate in the upload body, and curl's own conversion moves them across the literal boundary.\n\n## Affected versions\n\nTested and confirmed on commit `c2676bf9e639e31e44b12a2cc82cf2046231b056` (curl 8.22.0-DEV) and curl 8.6.0. The mismatch likely dates back to IMAP APPEND's introduction in 7.30.0.\n\n## Root cause\n\nIn `lib/imap.c`, `imap_perform_append()` sets up the upload reader with the original size and sends it in the IMAP literal:\n\n```c\nresult = Curl_creader_set_fread(data, data->state.infilesize);\n...\nresult = imap_sendf(data, imapc, \"APPEND %s%s {%\" FMT_OFF_T \"}\",\n                    mailbox, curlx_dyn_ptr(&flags), data->state.infilesize);\n```\n\nThe `cr-lineconv` reader (`lib/sendf.c`) converts each bare LF to CRLF and explicitly returns `-1` for its total length because the converted size is unknown. `Curl_req_send_more()` (`lib/request.c`) drains the reader until EOS — it does not stop at the IMAP-declared boundary.\n\nAn N-byte input containing k bare LFs produces N+k wire bytes while the server was told to consume only N.\n\n## Reproduction\n1. Build curl at commit c2676bf9e639e31e44b12a2cc82cf2046231b056 (or any version from 7.30.0 onward) on a Linux or macOS host, without TLS (plain IMAP is sufficient for reproduction).\n\n2. Create a 66-byte upload file consisting of A\\n repeated 22 times (44 bytes), followed by the 22-byte suffix \\nX CREATE \"Injected\"\\r\\n. The 22 bare LFs before the suffix are chosen so that CRLF expansion adds exactly 22 bytes — pushing the suffix past the declared literal boundary.\n\n3. Start a local IMAP server on port 11430 that implements standard IMAP framing: respond to CAPABILITY, LOGIN, and APPEND. On APPEND, parse the {N} literal size from the command, send + continue, then consume exactly N bytes from the socket as the literal body. After consuming the literal and its terminating CRLF, read and record the next line received (the first post-literal command).\n\n4. Run curl with CRLF conversion enabled, uploading the payload via IMAP APPEND:\n```\ncurl -sS --user user:pass --upload-file payload.bin --crlf \\\n  imap://127.0.0.1:11430/INBOX\n```\n\n5. Observe that curl exits with code 0 — it reports success.\n\n6. Check what the IMAP server received after the literal — it will have parsed X CREATE \"Injected\" as a standalone IMAP command, separate from the APPEND literal. This proves that bytes from the upload body escaped the declared literal boundary and were interpreted as IMAP protocol commands.\n\n7. Run a control case without --crlf using the same payload and an identical IMAP server on a different port. Without CRLF conversion, the entire 66-byte payload fits within the declared 66-byte literal. The first post-literal command will be curl's normal LOGOUT, and the X CREATE \"Injected\" text remains safely inside the message body.\n\n8. Compare the two cases — in both, curl declared a 66-byte literal in the APPEND command. With --crlf, the 22 bare LFs expanded to CRLFs, producing 88 wire bytes against a 66-byte declaration. The server consumed 66 bytes as the literal, and the remaining 22 bytes (\\r\\nX CREATE \"Injected\"\\r\\n) were parsed as protocol — the CRLF terminated the APPEND and X CREATE \"Injected\" executed as a new command.\n\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nReject the `CURLOPT_CRLF` / `CURLOPT_TRANSFERTEXT` + IMAP APPEND combination with an explicit error, or pre-compute the converted size before sending the APPEND literal declaration. Truncating output at the declared length would prevent command escape but silently corrupt the message — an explicit failure is preferable.\n\n## Impact\n\n## Summary:\n\nAn attacker who controls the content of an uploaded message can inject arbitrary IMAP commands when:\n\n- the application enables CRLF conversion (`CURLOPT_CRLF` / `--crlf`),\n- the upload has a known size (required by IMAP APPEND in curl),\n- curl performs an authenticated IMAP APPEND, and\n- the attacker can control the upload bytes with byte-level precision.\n\nThe injected commands execute with the authenticated IMAP user's full privileges (CREATE, DELETE, STORE, COPY, etc.). `CURLOPT_TRANSFERTEXT=1` on Unix reaches the same code path.\n\nConversion is disabled by default, which limits exposure, but the framing violation itself is unconditional whenever conversion is active.",
